<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Søren Kierkegaard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Søren Kierkegaard was a Danish philosopher and theologian, regarded as the first existentialist. Writing often under pseudonyms, in works such as &quot;Either/Or&quot; (1843) and &quot;Fear and Trembling&quot; (1843), he explored faith, choice, anxiety and the individual's relation to God. In his last years he attacked the established Danish church. His ideas became widely influential in the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soren-kierkegaard.jpg" alt="Søren Kierkegaard" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: La Biblioteca Real de Dinamarca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%B8ren_Kierkegaard_(1813-1855)_-_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Danish figures of wide influence include the philosopher <b>Søren Kierkegaard</b>, the writer Hans Christian Andersen and the physicist Niels Bohr, whose Copenhagen institute became a centre of quantum physics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Schopenhauer questioned earlier optimism, and <b>Søren Kierkegaard</b> and Friedrich Nietzsche rejected the idea that reality could be understood purely objectively.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Copenhagen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 265 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Søren Kierkegaard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 535h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
